Stryker experienced a major global cyberattack beginning March 11, 2026 that disrupted its corporate technology environment and materially affected business functions including order processing, manufacturing, shipping, and access to internal systems. Stryker initially described the disruption as concentrated in its Microsoft environment and later clarified that the affected scope included its broader corporate network. The company reported that connected medical products and patient-related services were not believed to have been compromised. Technical reporting describes Microsoft administrative infrastructure, including Intune, as central to the destructive activity, creating a case in which legitimate enterprise-management capabilities could effectively become attack primitives once administrative trust was compromised. Handala claimed responsibility and claimed more than 200,000 affected devices, but that scale and attribution should remain separately classified as threat-actor claims unless independently established. The incident is a major example of why identity, endpoint-management, and cloud administration systems must be treated as Tier-0 infrastructure.
